6.3.7 Facade Systems
Precast facade system shall be made up of following types of non-load bearing cladding panels:
a) Precast insulated concrete panels;
b) Precast solid cladding panels;
c) Architectural precast decorative facade panels; and d) Precast concrete fins.
All façade connections shall comply with the requirements of the good practice [C-7(18)] and 6.3.2.  Design shall account for differential movement, panel dead load and seismic restraint.

7.5.3 Moments and shears induced by line and concentrated loads shall be resisted by an effective section as
described in Fig. 14. If the total deck width, perpendicular to the span, is less than the span, modification may be PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION FIG. 13 CRITICAL SECTION FOR SHEAR
required. In addition, the following limitations would apply:
a) As the width of the system becomes narrower than the span length, the effective resisting widths will become narrower;
b) For extremely high span-depth ratios (more than approximately 50), the effective section at mid-span shall be reduced by 10 percent to 20 percent;
c) For spans less than about 3 m, the effective width at the support may become narrower; and d) Local load concentrations can cause longitudinal splitting failures due to transverse bending in the system and punching shear failure. The magnitude of concentrated loads shall be limited to minimise such failures.

7.6.1 Design Philosophy
The following factors shall be considered in designing for stability of a precast structure:
a) The overall structural system shall be checked for global and local stability under all applicable loading conditions including temporary and final stages;
b) Precast structural systems shall be designed to resist wind and all applicable lateral loads;
c) Cross walls or sway frames shall be so arranged to provide lateral stability;
d) When lateral loads are low, stability shall be verified using a notional horizontal force equal to 1.5 percent of total vertical load, as shown in Fig. 12;
e) Lateral stability shall be maintained during all construction and erection stages. Temporary bracing, shoring, or props shall be detailed and installed to match the staged loading conditions. A temporary NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026 All dimensions in millimetres.
FIG. 14 MOMENT AND SHEAR DISTRIBUTION WIDTHS FOR HCS
works scheme including propping layout, sequencing and dismantling plan shall be submitted and approved before execution;
f) Inverted T and L-beams shall be analysed for asymmetric loading during both construction and service stages, using all applicable load combinations;
g) The strength of grout shall be at least 50 percent of the design strength or as per design requirement before application of any other loads (temporary or permanent) on the element. Stress and cracking shall be evaluated and verified before proceeding;
h) Long-span and high-rise precast structures shall include engineered bracing and verified stability measures during all construction and service stages; and j) Temporary anchors shall be designed for proper alignment and safety of the elements. Appropriate washers shall be used.

7.7.1 Design Philosophy
The following factors shall be considered for achieving the design life and durability of a precast structure:
a) Geometry and detailing of precast elements;
b) Concrete mix design, including constituent materials;
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION
c) Nominal and minimum cover as per environmental class;
d) Environmental exposure classification in accordance with the good practice [C-7(2)];
e) Fire resistance rating based on intended use;
f) Access provisions for inspection and maintenance;
g) Controlled production quality;
h) Handling, transport and erection constraints; and j) Watertight and durable joint detailing.
In addition to above requirements, the recommendations specified in the good practice [C-7(2)] and relevant accepted standards shall be followed.

7.10.8.2 Calculation of diaphragm in-plane design moments, shears and axial forces shall be consistent with
requirements of equilibrium and with design boundary conditions. It shall be permitted to calculate design moments, shears, and axial forces in accordance with one of (a) through (d):
a) A rigid diaphragm model if the diaphragm can be idealized as rigid;
b) A flexible diaphragm model if the diaphragm can be idealized as flexible;
c) A bounding analysis in which the design values are the envelope of values obtained by assuming upper bound and lower bound in-plane stiffness for the diaphragm in two or more separate analyses; and d) A finite element model considering diaphragm flexibility (semi rigid).
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION FIG. 15 STRUCTURAL MODELS FOR FLOOR DIAPHRAGMS

7.11.14.3 The area of vertical shear reinforcement shall be calculated as follows:
Ash= (Avf+ An)fy μfys where Avf  = area of shear friction reinforcement;
An  = area of tension reinforcement;
fy    = yield strength of tension reinforcement;
μ    = coefficient of friction as per Table 6; and fys   = yield strength of shear reinforcement.

7.11.15.11 The anchorage of reinforcement shall be as per the following:
a) Horizontal bars As shall be extended a minimum of Ld beyond Crack 5 and anchored at the end of the component by welding to cross bars, plates, or angles;
b) Horizontal bars Ah shall be extended a minimum of Ld, beyond Crack 2 and anchored at the end of the component by hooks or other suitable means;
c) To ensure development of hanger reinforcement Ash, the rebar shall be bent and continued parallel to the bottom of the component, or a separate horizontal anchored reinforcement with A'sh ≥ Ash shall be provided. The extension of reinforcement (A’sh) at bottom of the component must be at least Ld beyond Crack 5. The A'sh, reinforcement may be anchored on the dap side by welding it to a plate, angle, or cross bar;
d) The beam flexure reinforcement may also be used to ensure development of Ash reinforcement, provided that the flexural reinforcement is adequately anchored on the dap side and the Ash reinforcement is wrapped around the flexural reinforcement;
e) In thin-stemmed components, it is recommended that the extension of the hanger reinforcement be greater than Ld. A’sh shall extend a distance that is the greater of 1.5 Ld of the strand past the face of the dap or 2.0 Ld of A‘sh beyond the diagonal Crack 5;
f) Vertical reinforcement Av should be properly anchored by hooks, as required by Part C/Section 5 of this NBCS;
g) Thin-stemmed components, except for the vertical ‘Z’ configuration, which has the bar welded to the plate, should have at least one 12 mm diameter rebar welded to the bearing plate or have equivalent development length (L-rebar); and h) Welded-wire reinforcement in place of bars may be used for reinforcement. It should be anchored in accordance with Part C ‘Structural Design, Section 5 Structural Concrete’ of this NBCS. In thinstemmed components the use of welded-wire reinforcement should be limited to situations where it is placed symmetrically in the width of the stem.

7.11.15.12 Other considerations
In addition to the reinforcement for the dapped end, it is important that the following be considered:
a) The depth of the extended end should not be less than about one half the depth of the beam, unless the beam is deeper than necessary for other than structural reasons;
b) The hanger reinforcement Ash should be placed as close as practical to the re-entrant corner and as symmetrically as possible around the centroid of the section being considered. This reinforcement requirement is not additive to other shear-reinforcement requirements but should be in addition to torsion reinforcement;
c) The shear strength of the full-depth beam behind the dap must be verified;
d) For thin-stemmed components, for a distance of 2H beyond the dap the concrete contribution to shear strength should be limited to:
Vc = 0.15√fckbwdp or Vc= 0.23√fckbwdp, where at least two bonded strands in the extended end are present where bw   =  average width of the stem for the full depth of the section; and dp   = distance from the top to the centroid of the prestressing strand for the full depth section and shall not be less than 0.8 H.
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION
e) For thin-stemmed components, for a distance 2 H beyond the dap, the reinforcement contribution to shear strength Vs shall be limited as per the following:
Vs= 0.15√fckbwdp

7.11.16.1 General
Beam and wall ledges (as shown in Fig. 25) shall be designed as per the following guidelines. Hanger steel requirements for the ledge in a wall need not be satisfied except where penetrations occur just below ledges. The design procedure explained in this section shall be applicable for the following cases:
a) Nu ≤ Vu;
b) Ledges with heights of 200 mm to 450 mm;
c) Ledge projections of 150 mm to 250 mm;
d) Bearing widths of components on the ledge ranging from 100 mm to 300 mm;
e) Distances of end load to end of ledge ranging from 100 mm to 900 mm; and f) Design concrete compressive strengths fck of 30 MPa to 80 MPa.
NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026 FIG. 25 DESIGN OF BEAM LEDGES

7.11.16.2 For shear strength of ledge for concentrated loads, not near the ends, where de ≥ 0.5 bt + hl + lp, a
symmetric failure controls the design punching shear strength of the ledge shall be determined as the lesser of following two equations:
VuR= 0.056γβ√fckhl(bt+ 2hl+ 2lp) or VuR= 0.028γβ√fckhl(bt+ 2hl+ s+ 2lp) For concentrated loads that are near ends, where de ≤ 0.5 bt + hl + lp, an asymmetric failure controls the design punching shear strength of the ledge and shall be as per the following:
VuR= 0.056γβ√fckhl(0.5bt+ hl+ de+ lp) or VuR= 0.56γβ√fckhl(0.5bt+ hl+ de+ lp) where γ  = √1 + 12.5 fpc fck, a factor for the effect of prestressing, 1 for precast beams;
β    = coefficient of shear strength based on R (as per Fig. 26);
hl   = height of beam ledge;
bt   = bearing width;
lp   = length of ledge projection;
s    = minimum spacing of concentrated loads;
de  = distance from centre of load to the end of the ledge; and fpc  = average prestress force after losses divided by gross concrete area of beam.

7.14.6 Lifting Inserts
The number of lifting inserts and location of inserts shall be decided by the following:
a) Lifting insert capacity (safe working load);
b) Total weight of the element;
c) Strength of concrete at age of lifting;
d) Shape of the unit;
e) Location of the inserts so that the failure of any one insert does not cause failure of the entire lifting system thereby ensuring the element can still be safely supported;
f) Position of any cut-outs and/or openings;
g) Rigging arrangement; and h) Anchorage of inserts.
All lifting inserts shall be designed by accounting the factors of safety given in Table 10. Typically lifting inserts shall be designed with a factor of safety of 4.
Table 10 Recommended Factors of Safety for Lifting Inserts (Table 7 and Clause 7.14.6) Sl No.
Item Recommended Factor of Safety (1) (2) (3) i) Bracing members ii) Bracing connections iii) Bracing inserts cast into precast members iv) Lifting inserts, normal circumstances v) Lifting inserts, multiple usage vi) Rebar inserts, single usage vii) Rebar inserts, multiple usage

8.1 Basic Design Considerations
A joint shall be considered based on the action of force transfer (tension, compression, shear, moment, etc) at an interface (one or many) between the precast components. A connection, which is a zone that includes various joints, shall be designed in combination of any of the following forces:
a) Compressive force;
b) Tensile force;
c) Shear force; and d) Flexural and torsional moments, as applicable.

8.1.1.8 Connections shall be provided with reinforcement as follows or with equivalent connection:
a) For walls elements, dowels shall be placed at a maximum spacing of 1/4th of the length of the wall panels or 450 mm or as required by design.  Also, at least two extreme edge dowels shall be continuous from foundation till roof using Class H coupler as per [C-7(9)] or any other connection;
b) For column minimum four corner bars of 12 mm diameter or as per design requirements shall be continuous from foundation till roof using Class H coupler as per [C-7(9)] or any other connection;
c) For beam at least one 12 mm dowel at centre of the bearing region of the beam shall be provided to avoid accidental failure; and d) For any other member appropriate reinforcement shall be provided to avoid accidental lateral loads.

8.1.4.3 Continuity within the joint may be obtained by any of the following or combination:
a) Lapping of bars;
b) Reinforcement grouted into apertures;
c) Overlapping loop or U-bars;
d) Reinforcement connected using threaded couplers; and e) Any other type of connection with appropriate testing of the connection.

8.3 Design of Connections
The design of connections shall follow those design methods and considerations for reinforced concrete, prestressed concrete and structural steel. Proprietary connections shall be based on tests and approved from competent authority or institutes of repute. The following types are the broad category of the connections based on connecting members:
a) Column to foundation;
b) Column to column;
c) Beam to column;
d) Beam to beam;
e) Beam to slab;
f) Beam to wall;
g) Wall to wall;
h) Wall to slab; and j) Slab to slab.
Examples of typical connections are shown in Annex E. These connections are for illustrative purposes only.  In all instances, the design should demonstrate that the connections are able to resist the applied structural actions.
Where appropriate, the suitability of the connection to act as a pinned or moment connection is highlighted. The location of the connections for a framed structure shall be as per 6 (Fig. 2, Fig. 3, Fig. 4 and Fig. 5).

8.3.1 Detailing of Reinforcement
The following shall be considered:
a) Lapping of bars — Where bars passing through the connection are lapped to provide continuity of reinforcement, the recommendations good practice [C-7(2)] and integrity tie requirements shall be followed;
b) Reinforcement grouted into aperture — An adequate capacity should be provided for grouted reinforcing bars to prevent pull-out. Embedment length shall be as per manufacturer guidelines, however testing shall be carried out as per 12. If grouted tube is used, the embedment length of dowel shall be at least 300 mm or as required by design, whichever is higher;
c) Reinforcement loops — Loop reinforcement shall be provided as per 8.1.2 and Part C ‘Structural Design, Section 5 Structural Concrete’ of this NBCS;
d) Couplers — Reinforcement may be connected by couplers. The concrete cover to the couplers (other than grouted tube or duct) shall be at least 25 mm or as per specifications of the project. A locking device should be used for threaded coupler connections where there is a risk of the threaded connection working loose, for example during vibration of in-situ concrete. Reference should be made to [C-7(9)] and the manufacturers’ technical specifications for guidance and acceptance criteria for the usage. Testing of couplers shall be as per 12 and relevant guidelines of [C-7(9)]. When the system is part of lateral force resisting system, the grout strength shall not be less than 90 MPa and testing shall be carried out as per 12 by the supplier of the coupler or as agreed upon by the project team. Modify to mention 12 for LFRS and 7(9) for regular coupler without lateral force; and PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION
e) Welding of bars — When bars are connected by welding, the connection shall ensure full capacity of the bar is achieved and failure of weld is avoided. Testing of the welded samples shall be as per relevant standards.

8.3.5.1 Provision of bond, anchorage, bearing, laps, joints and bend in bars shall be as follows:
a) Avoidance of bond failure due to ultimate loads — At both sides of any cross-section the force in each bar should be developed by an appropriate embedment length or other end anchorage;
b) Anchorage bond stress — Anchorage bond stress (τb) is assumed to be constant over the effective anchorage length. It may be taken as the force in the bar divided by its effective surface anchorage area, as given below:
τb= Fs πφEL where τb  = bond stress;
Fs   = force in the bar or group of bars;
φE  = effective bar size which, for a single bar is equal to the bar size and, for a group of bars;
and L     = anchorage length.
NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026
c) Values for design ultimate anchorage bond stress — Values for design ultimate anchorage bond stress τbu may be obtained from the equation.  The anchorage bond stress (τb) shall not exceed the design ultimate anchorage bond stress:
τbu= β√fcu where τbu = design ultimate anchorage bond stress; and β    = a coefficient dependent on the bar type.
The values of β may be taken from Table 11. These values include a partial safety factor (γm) of 1.4.
Table 11 Values of Bond Coefficient β [Clause 8.3.5.1(c)] Sl No.
Bar Type β Bar in Tension Bar in Compression (1) (2) (3) (4) i) Plain bar 0.28 0.35 ii) Deformed bar 0.4 0.63

8.5.7 Ties between Precast Element and Topping
In potential plastic hinge regions, the consequences of delamination of the topping from the precast members shall be assessed.  If the maximum shear stress at any section exceeds 0.4 MPa, connectors shall be provided between the topping and precast to achieve composite action as per the following guidelines:
a) Ties with an effective area of 40 mm2 per m2 of floor area, or equivalent connectors, shall connect the topping to the precast element;
b) Spacing of connectors shall not exceed 1 500 mm and the tributary area of topping reliant on each connector shall not exceed 2.25 m2;
c) Connectors shall engage horizontal reinforcement, or shall be otherwise effectively anchored into both the topping and the precast element, or into the joints between precast elements; and d) In HCS appropriate connection shall be placed in slab joints.

8.5.11 Progressive Collapse
The following recommendations shall be followed for ensuring stability of prefabricated structures:
a) Adequate strengthening of external joints of external wall panels is important since these elements are not fully restrained on both sides by floor panels. Adequate design precautions shall be taken. PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION FIG. 38 CONTINUITY OF TIES: BARS LAPPED WITHIN IN-SITU CONCRETE
Connections for the external wall panels are likely to be the weakest points of a precast panel building, if not designed properly;
b) Restraint shall be provided to all load bearing elements at the corners of the building. These elements and the external ends of cross-wall units shall be stiffened either by introducing columns as connecting units or by jointing them to non-structural wall units, which in emergency shall act in force transfer;
c) In prefabricated construction, the possibility of gas or other explosions which can damage primary structural elements leading to progressive collapse of the structure shall be considered. It is, therefore, necessary to consider the possibility of progressive collapse in which the failure or displacement of one element of a structure causes the failure or displacement of another element and results in the partial or total collapse of the building;
d) Provision in the design to reduce the probability of progressive collapse is essential in buildings of height more than 15 m and is of relatively higher priority than for buildings of lower height. However, buildings of special importance shall be checked for progressive collapse irrespective of height guidelines.

One way of preventing progressive collapse of external wall panels is to design the combined precast wall panels of entire width as a beam of depth equal to the floor height for the impact loading of upper floors and provide reinforcement accordingly at the top and bottom of all precast wall panels at each floor. To make reinforcement continuous across joints, top and bottom bars projected into joints between wall panels shall be joined with lapping bars;
e) It is necessary to ensure that any local damage to a structure does not spread to other parts of the structure remote from the point of mishap and that the overall stability is not impaired, but it may not be necessary to stiffen all parts of the structure against local damage or collapse in the immediate vicinity of a mishap, unless the design briefs specially require this to be done. The requirements as specified at 8.5 shall be followed to prevent progressive collapse of the structure;
f) Additional protection may be required in respect of damage from vehicles. Further, it is necessary to consider the effect of damage to or displacement of a load-bearing member by an uncontrolled vehicle. It is strongly recommended that important structural members are adequately protected by concrete kerbs or similar method. In areas with possibility of passenger vehicular impact, the precast components shall be designed for an un-factored load of 27 kN, in any direction, acting at heights between 450 mm and
700 mm on an area not larger than 300 mm × 300 mm to produce maximum load effect. This load is not
required to act concurrently with any handrail or guard rail system loads; and g) In all aspects of erection that affect structural design, it is essential that the designer shall maintain a close liaison with the builder/contractor regarding the erection procedures to be followed.

9.1.1 Importance of Pre-Engineering
Pre-engineering plays a critical role in the planning and execution of any precast concrete project. To ensure a successful outcome, the planning engineer should begin working from the design stage to ensure proper production, installation, connections, and logistics. Key resources must be identified and prepared for each phase of the project, including:
a) Availability land/approvals/water and electricity for a site-based factory;
NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026
b) Suitable plant and machinery;
c) Steel moulds;
d) Lifting machinery at factory;
e) Trailers;
f) Lifting machinery on site (tower cranes/mobile cranes); and g) Site access for transportation of large elements as well as route studies and local regulations and hours for operating large trailers.

9.1.2 Resource Planning and Feasibility Factors
The resources should be carefully planned based on the types of project elements and their quantities. The engineer is also responsible for reserving these resources throughout the project duration.  Key feasibility considerations include:
a) Building geometry — Ensure shapes and geometry are as symmetrical and regular as possible;
b) Grid-lines — Column and wall locations should, preferably, follow a standardized pattern;
c) Height clearances — Consideration of clear height expectations between beams and slabs;
d) Grid spacing — Optimize grid spacing to take full advantage of precast elements; and e) Standardization — Standardize elements within one floor and across multiple floors when feasible.

9.1.3 Pre-Engineering Design Stage Process
The pre-engineering design stage should include the following steps:
a) Study of building functionality;
b) Load assumptions and calculations;
c) Preparation of the design basis report (DBR);
d) Structural analysis;
e) Preliminary framing plan selection for precast elements;
f) Approval of preliminary framing plans by the architect/client;
g) Review meetings with architects and consultants;
h) Finalization of the structural framing plan after incorporating feedback and value engineering;
j) Release of the good for construction (GFC) and structural framing plan (R0), including:
1) Geometry of precast elements;
2) General arrangement (GA) drawings with reinforcement details; and 3) Connections and inserts.
k) Coordination with mechanical, electrical, plumbing and other service consultants should occur during the early stages.

9.2.1 Production Planning
Careful planning is essential for ensuring that precast concrete systems are produced efficiently.  Key factors to address include:
a) Synchronization of manufacturing schedules and resources to meet construction and storage needs;
b) Anticipation of any special transportation or access challenges;
c) Use of detailed production shop drawings to facilitate smooth execution; and d) Factory curing periods for high strength concrete elements.
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION

9.2.1.1 Shop drawings
Shop drawings should contain:
a) Member shapes (elevations and sections) and dimensions;
b) Details of reinforcement, anchors, inserts, etc;
c) Connection details;
d) Production tolerances;
e) Handling and lifting devices;
f) Finishing and bearing seat details;
g) Transportation and storage methods; and h) Unique identification of elements (ID, location and orientation).

9.2.1.2 Factory vs on-site production
A decision whether precast elements should be produced in an off-site factory or on-site, based on factors such as:
a) Project scale and volume;
b) Availability of factory space near the site;
c) Contractual agreements regarding space provision;
d) Element type, weight, and size; and e) Cost-benefit analysis.

9.2.2.2 Developing the production schedule
A production schedule developed based on the following:
a) Splitting the floor plan into various ‘installation parts’, typically aligned with crane locations and capacities;
b) Estimation the number of elements per part and the installation duration for each part based on element type and connection complexity;
c) Using historical data and experience to estimate installation cycle times for each element;
d) Assigning unique element IDs to each installation part and sequence them accordingly;
e) Publishing a sequence chart that includes:
1) Planned drawing release date;
2) Planned casting date;
3) Planned transportation date; and 4) Planned installation date.
NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026

9.3.1 Haulage Planning
Transportation constraints influence the size and shape of precast elements.  The following factors should be considered in haulage planning:
a) Trailer lengths (standard lengths of 6 m, 12 m, with special cases up to 30 m);
b) Trailer width (typically between 2.4 m to 2.7 m);
c) Height clearance (3.6 m to 4 m, adjustable with low-bed trailers);
d) Weight capacity (25 MT to 35 MT);
e) Special permits for oversized loads that may be needed;
f) Route surveys and permissions to be obtained if crossing bridges or navigating urban areas; and g) Large element transportation time slots through day/night as per local regulations.

9.3.3 Crane Planning
A comprehensive crane layout should be developed in collaboration with the installation planning engineers considering the following:
a) Types of cranes (tower cranes, mobile cranes);
b) Site constraints (space, access);
c) Ground strata for crane footing;
d) Lifting capacities and feeding locations;
e) Crane removal and repositioning areas; and f) Double handling constraints due to storage at site or crane reach.

9.4 Installation Sequence
Once the casting sequence is set, installation dates to each element should be assigned based on factors such as:
a) Crane type and operational speed;
b) Reduced crane efficiency where multiple cranes are deployed with overlapping zones;
c) Element type and installation cycle time;
d) Connection complexity;
e) Grouting and curing durations;
f) The number of shifts and crane operation hours; and g) Adjustment of installation dates and resources as needed based on available machinery and logistics.

9.5.1 Surveying
The first activity on-site is surveying and line-out.  It should be ensured that key points are marked, including:
a) Building corners and faces;
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION
b) Compound wall boundaries;
c) Grid lines and column centres;
d) Footing corners;
e) Shear wall grid lines; and f) Site constraints such as high-tension lines or existing canals or water bodies or filled up areas or marsh lands.

10.1.3.1 Core process
The main processes that include the following steps:
a) Providing and assembling moulds, positioning the reinforcement cage for reinforced concrete work and stressing the wires for prestressed elements;
b) Installing concealed service conduits/pipes;
c) Fixing inserts and tubes as necessary (for handling purposes);
d) Pouring concrete into the moulds;
e) Vibrating and finishing the concrete;
f) Curing the concrete; and g) Demoulding the forms and stacking the precast products.

10.1.3.2 Auxiliary process
These processes are necessary for the successful completion of the core processes and include the following:
a) Mixing and producing fresh concrete (carried out in a mixing station or batching plant);
b) Prefabricating the reinforcement cage (performed in a steel yard or workshop);
c) Manufacturing inserts and other finishing components to be incorporated into the main precast products;
and d) Finishing the precast products.

10.1.3.3 Subsidiary process
Subsidiary processes shall include material handling, maintenance and utility services essential for uninterrupted production. These shall include:
a) Proper storage of raw materials;
b) Internal transport systems for fresh concrete and cages;
c) Safe movement and stacking of precast elements;
d) Periodic servicing of equipment and moulds; and e) Maintenance of curing and utility facilities (for example, steam).
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION

10.1.4 The manufacturing of precast elements requires systematic planning of all the processes to achieve the
following objectives:
a) A cyclic working method to enhance speed and cost-efficiency in production;
b) Mechanization of processes to boost productivity and improve quality;
c) Optimal production levels that meet quality control standards while maintaining the desired construction speed;
d) Improved working conditions for the workforce; and e) Minimization of weather-related disruptions to the manufacturing schedule.

10.3.8 Other Methods of Accelerated Hardening
Accelerated hardening of concrete may also be achieved by one or more of the following techniques:
a) Use of Construction Chemicals — Suitable construction chemicals may be used for promoting early hardening;
b) Consolidation by Spinning — This method shall generally be employed in centrifugal moulding of pipes and similar units. The spinning action removes excess water, effects consolidation, and permits earlier demoulding;
c) Pressed Concrete — This method shall be suitable for the fabrication of small or large products at high rates of production. Presses in the range of 100 t to 200 t capacity shall be used to compress the wet concrete in rigid moulds, thereby expelling excess water. This results in early handling and produces dense, wear-resistant concrete;
d) Vacuum Treatment — In this method, surplus air and water shall be removed from the freshly placed concrete (for example, slabs and similar elements). A suction of up to 70 percent of an atmosphere shall be applied for a period of 20 min to 30 min per cm thickness of the units; and e) Consolidation by Shock — This method shall be suitable for small concrete units placed in strong moulds and subjected to repeated dropping from a height. The number of shocks required to expel excess water and air may vary from 6 to 20 and the height of drop may be up to half the depth of the mould.

10.3.9 Stacking During Transport and Storage
Every precaution shall be taken against overstress or damage, by the provision of suitable packing at agreed points of support. In general, the final condition of the elements in the structure should be studied to finalize on the staking support. Even number of supports is preferred over the odd number of supports. Particular attention is directed to the inherent dangers of breakage and damage caused by supporting other than at two positions, and by the careless placing of packing (for example, not vertically one above the other). Ribs, corners and intricate projections from solid section should be adequately protected. Packing pieces shall not stain, disfigure or otherwise cause permanent mark on units or members. Stacking shall be arranged, or the precast units protected, to prevent the accumulation of trapped water or rubbish and, if necessary, to reduce the risk of efflorescence. PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION
The following points shall be kept in view during stacking:
a) Care should be taken to ensure that the flat elements are stacked as per their final position at the structure. For identification, top surfaces should be clearly marked;
b) Stacking should be done on hard and suitable ground to avoid any sinking of support when elements are stacked;
c) In case of horizontal stacking, packing materials shall be at specified locations and shall be exactly one over the other to avoid cantilever stress in panels;
d) Components should be packed in a uniform way to avoid any undue projection of elements in the stack which normally is a source of accident;
e) In general, vertical stacking should be limited to 6 components to 10 components. Minimum ground capacity shall be 5 t/m2 for stacking of panels. Where the ground is only compacted, the maximum ground load at support should be restricted to 15 t/m2.

10.7.1 Tower cranes or other mobile cranes have varying lifting capacities at different radii, with the maximum
weight at a radius closer to the centre and the minimum weight at a radius farther from the centre.  To minimize the number of precast elements, the selected crane should be capable of handling the heaviest precast elements at its maximum radius.  The floor plan should indicate the weight of panels at different crane radii, ensuring they align with the crane's lifting capacity at each radius.
In the erection of precast elements, all the following items of work are meant to be included:
a) Slinging of the precast element;
b) Tying up of erection ropes connecting to the erection hooks;
c) Cleaning of the elements and the site of erection;
d) Cleaning of the steel inserts before incorporation in the joints, lifting of the elements, setting them down into the correct envisaged position;
e) Adjustment to get the stipulated level, line and plumb by means of levelling bolts and adjustable props;
f) Welding of cleats;
g) Changing of the erection tackles;
h) Putting up and removing the necessary scaffolding or supports;
j) Welding of the inserts, laying of reinforcements in joints and grouting the joints; and k) Finishing the joints to bring the whole work to be like finished product.
