9.3 Project and Factory Logistics Planning
9.3.1 Haulage Planning
Transportation constraints influence the size and shape of precast elements.  The following factors should be considered in haulage planning:
a) Trailer lengths (standard lengths of 6 m, 12 m, with special cases up to 30 m);
b) Trailer width (typically between 2.4 m to 2.7 m);
c) Height clearance (3.6 m to 4 m, adjustable with low-bed trailers);
d) Weight capacity (25 MT to 35 MT);
e) Special permits for oversized loads that may be needed;
f) Route surveys and permissions to be obtained if crossing bridges or navigating urban areas; and g) Large element transportation time slots through day/night as per local regulations.
9.3.2 Factory Stockyard and Logistics Coordination
Precast elements in the factory stockyard should be stored using first in, first out (FIFO) or last in, first out (LIFO) methods, depending on site requirements.  Proper stock registers and inventory management software should be used for efficient tracking and organization. Storage size of the yards should be defined by the minimum concrete strength and curing periods.
9.3.3 Crane Planning
A comprehensive crane layout should be developed in collaboration with the installation planning engineers considering the following:
a) Types of cranes (tower cranes, mobile cranes);
b) Site constraints (space, access);
c) Ground strata for crane footing;
d) Lifting capacities and feeding locations;
e) Crane removal and repositioning areas; and f) Double handling constraints due to storage at site or crane reach.
9.4 Installation Sequence
Once the casting sequence is set, installation dates to each element should be assigned based on factors such as:
a) Crane type and operational speed;
b) Reduced crane efficiency where multiple cranes are deployed with overlapping zones;
c) Element type and installation cycle time;
d) Connection complexity;
e) Grouting and curing durations;
f) The number of shifts and crane operation hours; and g) Adjustment of installation dates and resources as needed based on available machinery and logistics.
9.5 On Site Work Planning
9.5.1 Surveying
The first activity on-site is surveying and line-out.  It should be ensured that key points are marked, including:
a) Building corners and faces;
