ANNEX B
(Foreword, Clauses 6.5.1 and G-6.2.2) GUIDELINE FOR 3D PRINTED CONCRETE STRUCTURES B-1 GENERAL This annex provides an overview of the materials, methods and tests associated with concrete construction using 3D printing for various applications. The provisions contained herein are based on the current state of knowledge and practice in 3D printed concrete construction. In addition to these guidelines, reference shall be made to the applicable existing Indian standards and relevant research publications. Ongoing research at the national and international levels is expected to generate design-related data, which may subsequently be utilized for the purpose of standardization.
B-2 METHODOLOGY AND SCOPE The following points are considered while defining the application and performance guidelines:
a) Printing technique — This document considers only extrusion-based 3D printing technology, where layers are deposited one over the other to construct a structure;
b) Design freedom — 3D printing allows for flexibility in the geometry and form. The shape, size and topology of any 3D printed concrete element for any application shall not be restricted.  However, the performance requirements for 3D printing need to be satisfied;
c) Material — The material shall be printable and buildable. As per the current state of knowledge of concrete for 3D printing, the limits on the usage of cementitious materials as given in good practice [C-7(6)] and Part C ‘Structural Design, Section 5 Structural Concrete’ of this NBCS would not be applicable for 3D printed concrete. The assessment of 3D printability of any concrete mixture needs to be carried out as per the available specialist literature on the subject. The mechanical, durability and long-term properties of the printable material shall be determined, as applicable.  In the absence of a well-established procedure for preparation of test specimens for such evaluations, reference shall be made to recognized specialized literature. The testing shall be conducted at accredited laboratories and approved by competent authority;
d) Performance criteria — The performance criteria of any 3D printed components shall conform to the relevant Indian Standards for equivalent components.  In the absence of any standards, an experimentbased approach shall be followed to satisfy the requirements for the specific element or component; and e) Special applications — In the absence of any relevant standard, the engineer-in-charge, authorised to define the qualification requirements in accordance with the applicable Indian Standards, shall take decisions based on sound engineering judgement and established practice.  The testing shall be conducted at accredited laboratories and approved by competent authority.
B-3 APPLICATION — NON-LOAD BEARING B-3.1 Falsework/Formwork 3D-printed concrete may be used as stay-in-place (permanent) or temporary formwork for new concrete construction. The structural integrity of the formwork shall be either certified by a practicing structural engineer before the application or the formwork shall satisfy deformation limits and structural requirement mentioned in good practice [C-7(26)]. The formwork shall be designed by testing. The stay in place formwork shall not be considered as a part of the structural member.
B-3.2 3D Printed Concrete Building Blocks The blocks can be solid as well as hollow, following the definition in accepted standard [C-7(27)]. The blocks shall have any external dimension greater than the corresponding dimension of a brick as specified in accepted standard [C-7(28)]. The size and the mass shall be such that it can be handled by as single person. In case of mechanized placement of the blocks, larger units can be used. The height of the block shall not exceed either its PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION

