D-7 DESIGN OF CONNECTIONS Design methods for joints in precast construction have been covered by various international technical journals and published academic research papers.  They briefly cover the connections listed below:
a) Corbel-beam end joint — A hidden mechanical connector can be used for the corbel-beam end joint, similar to hanger connections;
b) Column/wall base mechanical connections — Column shoes and wall shoes with anchor bolts can be used for mechanical connections at the base of columns and walls;
c) Wall to wall connections — Wire loops can be used for wall to wall connections;
d) Beam to beam — Steel hangers can be used for connecting precast concrete beams; and e) Rigid beam-column — Top steel couplers and bottom steel welded connections can be used for rigid beam-column connections, ensuring that the connection is strong and can resist bending and shear forces.
D-8 CONNECTION DESIGN CONCEPT This can cover the following:
a) Load paths — Diaphragm, slab to wall/column paths, slab-beam shear connection;
b) Frame behaviours;
c) Accidental loads, progressive collapse;
d) Tie design forces;
e) Tie continuity — Laps, tie with enclosed links; and f) Tie anchorages with other ties.
