9.3.1 Haulage Planning
Transportation constraints influence the size and shape of precast elements.  The following factors should be considered in haulage planning:
a) Trailer lengths (standard lengths of 6 m, 12 m, with special cases up to 30 m);
b) Trailer width (typically between 2.4 m to 2.7 m);
c) Height clearance (3.6 m to 4 m, adjustable with low-bed trailers);
d) Weight capacity (25 MT to 35 MT);
e) Special permits for oversized loads that may be needed;
f) Route surveys and permissions to be obtained if crossing bridges or navigating urban areas; and g) Large element transportation time slots through day/night as per local regulations.

9.3.3 Crane Planning
A comprehensive crane layout should be developed in collaboration with the installation planning engineers considering the following:
a) Types of cranes (tower cranes, mobile cranes);
b) Site constraints (space, access);
c) Ground strata for crane footing;
d) Lifting capacities and feeding locations;
e) Crane removal and repositioning areas; and f) Double handling constraints due to storage at site or crane reach.

9.4 Installation Sequence
Once the casting sequence is set, installation dates to each element should be assigned based on factors such as:
a) Crane type and operational speed;
b) Reduced crane efficiency where multiple cranes are deployed with overlapping zones;
c) Element type and installation cycle time;
d) Connection complexity;
e) Grouting and curing durations;
f) The number of shifts and crane operation hours; and g) Adjustment of installation dates and resources as needed based on available machinery and logistics.

9.5.1 Surveying
The first activity on-site is surveying and line-out.  It should be ensured that key points are marked, including:
a) Building corners and faces;
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION
b) Compound wall boundaries;
c) Grid lines and column centres;
d) Footing corners;
e) Shear wall grid lines; and f) Site constraints such as high-tension lines or existing canals or water bodies or filled up areas or marsh lands.

10.1.3.1 Core process
The main processes that include the following steps:
a) Providing and assembling moulds, positioning the reinforcement cage for reinforced concrete work and stressing the wires for prestressed elements;
b) Installing concealed service conduits/pipes;
c) Fixing inserts and tubes as necessary (for handling purposes);
d) Pouring concrete into the moulds;
e) Vibrating and finishing the concrete;
f) Curing the concrete; and g) Demoulding the forms and stacking the precast products.

10.1.3.2 Auxiliary process
These processes are necessary for the successful completion of the core processes and include the following:
a) Mixing and producing fresh concrete (carried out in a mixing station or batching plant);
b) Prefabricating the reinforcement cage (performed in a steel yard or workshop);
c) Manufacturing inserts and other finishing components to be incorporated into the main precast products;
and d) Finishing the precast products.

10.1.3.3 Subsidiary process
Subsidiary processes shall include material handling, maintenance and utility services essential for uninterrupted production. These shall include:
a) Proper storage of raw materials;
b) Internal transport systems for fresh concrete and cages;
c) Safe movement and stacking of precast elements;
d) Periodic servicing of equipment and moulds; and e) Maintenance of curing and utility facilities (for example, steam).
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION

10.1.4 The manufacturing of precast elements requires systematic planning of all the processes to achieve the
following objectives:
a) A cyclic working method to enhance speed and cost-efficiency in production;
b) Mechanization of processes to boost productivity and improve quality;
c) Optimal production levels that meet quality control standards while maintaining the desired construction speed;
d) Improved working conditions for the workforce; and e) Minimization of weather-related disruptions to the manufacturing schedule.

10.3.8 Other Methods of Accelerated Hardening
Accelerated hardening of concrete may also be achieved by one or more of the following techniques:
a) Use of Construction Chemicals — Suitable construction chemicals may be used for promoting early hardening;
b) Consolidation by Spinning — This method shall generally be employed in centrifugal moulding of pipes and similar units. The spinning action removes excess water, effects consolidation, and permits earlier demoulding;
c) Pressed Concrete — This method shall be suitable for the fabrication of small or large products at high rates of production. Presses in the range of 100 t to 200 t capacity shall be used to compress the wet concrete in rigid moulds, thereby expelling excess water. This results in early handling and produces dense, wear-resistant concrete;
d) Vacuum Treatment — In this method, surplus air and water shall be removed from the freshly placed concrete (for example, slabs and similar elements). A suction of up to 70 percent of an atmosphere shall be applied for a period of 20 min to 30 min per cm thickness of the units; and e) Consolidation by Shock — This method shall be suitable for small concrete units placed in strong moulds and subjected to repeated dropping from a height. The number of shocks required to expel excess water and air may vary from 6 to 20 and the height of drop may be up to half the depth of the mould.

10.3.9 Stacking During Transport and Storage
Every precaution shall be taken against overstress or damage, by the provision of suitable packing at agreed points of support.  In general, the final condition of the elements in the structure should be studied to finalize on the staking support.  Even number of supports is preferred over the odd number of supports.  Particular attention is directed to the inherent dangers of breakage and damage caused by supporting other than at two positions, and by the careless placing of packing (for example, not vertically one above the other). Ribs, corners and intricate projections from solid section should be adequately protected.  Packing pieces shall not stain, disfigure or otherwise cause permanent mark on units or members. Stacking shall be arranged, or the precast units protected, to prevent the accumulation of trapped water or rubbish and, if necessary, to reduce the risk of efflorescence.
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION
The following points shall be kept in view during stacking:
a) Care should be taken to ensure that the flat elements are stacked as per their final position at the structure.
For identification, top surfaces should be clearly marked;
b) Stacking should be done on hard and suitable ground to avoid any sinking of support when elements are stacked;
c) In case of horizontal stacking, packing materials shall be at specified locations and shall be exactly one over the other to avoid cantilever stress in panels;
d) Components should be packed in a uniform way to avoid any undue projection of elements in the stack which normally is a source of accident;
e) In general, vertical stacking should be limited to 6 components to 10 components.  Minimum ground capacity shall be 5 t/m2 for stacking of panels. Where the ground is only compacted, the maximum ground load at support should be restricted to 15 t/m2.  Where the ground is of PCC layer, the maximum ground load at support should be restricted to 30 t/m2 subjected to capacity of the ground. In both cases, the ground should be levelled, and the support should be continuous along the width of the panels; and f) All wall panels shall be stacked, preferably symmetrically on both sides, against steel A-frames. The slope of the sides of the A-frame shall be between 75° and 80° to the horizontal and the bottom of the frame shall be provided with concrete footings to resist component of self- weight and horizontal wind pressure on the wall panel. A similar type of A-frame should be used for transporting wall panels.

10.7.1 Tower cranes or other mobile cranes have varying lifting capacities at different radii, with the maximum
weight at a radius closer to the centre and the minimum weight at a radius farther from the centre.  To minimize the number of precast elements, the selected crane should be capable of handling the heaviest precast elements at its maximum radius.  The floor plan should indicate the weight of panels at different crane radii, ensuring they align with the crane's lifting capacity at each radius.
In the erection of precast elements, all the following items of work are meant to be included:
a) Slinging of the precast element;
b) Tying up of erection ropes connecting to the erection hooks;
c) Cleaning of the elements and the site of erection;
d) Cleaning of the steel inserts before incorporation in the joints, lifting of the elements, setting them down into the correct envisaged position;
e) Adjustment to get the stipulated level, line and plumb by means of levelling bolts and adjustable props;
f) Welding of cleats;
g) Changing of the erection tackles;
h) Putting up and removing the necessary scaffolding or supports;
j) Welding of the inserts, laying of reinforcements in joints and grouting the joints; and k) Finishing the joints to bring the whole work to be like finished product.
See 10.8 for additional design considerations.

10.7.4 For Travelling Cranes
a) The travel tracks shall be perfectly levelled both longitudinally and transversally;
b) The gauge shall be constant and the rail tracks perfectly straight and with the same shape along the entire path;
c) The rail tracks shall be placed on a solid base;
PART C  STRUCTURAL DESIGN — SECTION 7 PREFABRICATED CONCRETE CONSTRUCTION
d) Travelling stop buffers shall be placed at the ends of the rail tracks;
e) For rail shape tolerances, the OEM’s installation manual shall be referred; and f) The track of the crane should be checked daily to see that all fish plates and bolts connecting them to the sleepers are in place and in good condition.

10.9.1 The strategic equipment used in the precast concrete industry/construction may be classified into the
following categories:
a) Production equipment — Batching plant, concrete shuttle conveyors concrete vibrators, circulation tables, EOT, gantry, shear lines, stirrup making machines, weld mesh machines, robots;
b) Erection equipment — Tower cranes, Mobile cranes, derricks, hoists, chain pulley blocks, etc;
c) Transport machinery — Tractor-cum-trailers, dumpers, lorries, locomotives and rarely even helicopters;
d) Workshop machinery — For making and repairing steel and timber moulds;
e) Minor tools and tackles, such as lifting equipment, electric vehicle-dumpers, concrete buckets, etc; and f) Steam generation plant for accelerated curing.
Each of the above groups may further be classified into various categories of machines and further to various other types depending on the source of power and capacity. For use of renewable energy sources to achieve sustainability in precast construction G-3 shall be referred.

10.10.1 Combinations of System Components for Mixed and Composite Construction
The following combinations may be used in mixed and composite construction:
a) Structural steel work and timber roofs on precast frames;
b) Precast half slab, solid slabs, hollow core slabs (floors) onto steel and concrete beams and masonry walls;
c) Profiled metal decking on precast beams;
d) Cast in situ slab on precast wall;
e) Precast slab on cast in situ wall/3D printed wall;
f) Precast frames onto cast in-situ foundations, retaining walls, etc;
g) Precast frames stabilized by masonry walls, steel bracing, etc;
h) Precast cladding in steel or cast in-situ frames and vice-versa;
j) Glass curtain walling, stone cladding or metal sheeting onto precast concrete frames, etc; and k) Reinforced concrete and structural steel as composite columns and beams.
10.10.2 Precast concrete may be combined with cast in-situ concrete, often termed hybrid construction. Cast insitu is mostly used to form homogenous connections between precast elements and provide a structural topping
for horizontal diaphragm action. In other cases, it is used to form the foundations and sub-structure to the building.

10.10.6 Design Considerations
The mixed and composite structures shall be analysed appropriately and the joints in them designed to take the forces of an equivalent discrete system. Resistance to horizontal loading shall be provided by placing beams, walls and/or bracings in two directions at right angles or otherwise.  The individual components shall be designed, taking into consideration appropriate end conditions and loads at various stages of construction.  The components of the structure shall be designed for loads in accordance with good practices [C-7(1)] and[C-7(20)] and 5 of Part C ‘Structural Design, Section 1 Loads and Load Effects’ of this NBCS.  In addition, members shall be designed for handling, erection and impact loads that may be expected during handling and erection.
For mixed and composite construction, the following shall be considered:
a) Positions of stability cores, walls, bracing, etc — In high rise buildings, the most popular method is a central core constructed several storeys ahead of the framework. The core could be built using either precast concrete panels, or cast in-situ walls, or steel cross bracing or precast concrete diagonal bracing;
b) Maturity of connections — Cast-in-situ grouted joints need temporary propping until desired design strength is achieved unless combined mechanical connections are also used. The overall construction sequence should be planned ahead of time carefully as per the design requirements. The design of temporary bracing system using key components should be looked at critically;
NATIONAL BUILDING CONSTRUCTION STANDARDS 2026
c) Availability and/or positioning of equipment to transport and erect components — The size and weight of the various components shall be organized to make optimum use of crane capacity, for example, the lightest units farthest from the operating zone;
d) Erection safety and speed of construction, with attention to cast in-situ concreting sequences — This is particularly important where fixing gangs are unaccustomed to working with different materials; and e) Tolerances for construction — This is particularly important where different manufacturers are producing components in different materials.
Other design considerations and safety requirements against progressive collapse shall be in accordance with this NBCS.

