5.2.9.5 Mean radiant temperature (MRT)
Besides solar energy transmittance, heat gain and losses occur by radiation exchange between the glazing which influences thermal comfort of the occupants.  Mean radiant temperature (MRT), which is defined as the uniform temperature of an imaginary enclosure in which radiant heat transfer from the human body is equal to the radiant heat transfer in the actual non-uniform enclosure, is used as a metric in thermal comfort assessment.  MRT can be calculated by various methods, among which measuring with globe thermometer is the most common and is given by:
